{"term":"reform","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:dc304b95f0f91a1e66","definition":"The change of something that is defective, broken, inefficient or otherwise negative, in order to correct or improve it","example":"The elections need to undergo a serious reform.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["reformation","amendment","rectification","correction"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":["counterreform","misreform"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/reform#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:7da1e9469250c2a622","definition":"To put into a new and improved form or condition; to restore to a former good state, or bring from bad to good; to change from worse to better.","example":"to reform a profligate man; to reform corrupt manners or morals; to reform a criminal","pos":"verb","synonyms":["amend","correct","rectify","mend","repair","better","improve","restore","reclaim"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":["counterreform","misreform","reformability","reformable","reformate","reformee","Reformer","rereform","unreformability"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/reform#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:3827e5893397c78a1c","definition":"To return to a good state; to amend or correct one's own character or habits.","example":"It is hoped that many criminals, upon being freed, will eventually reform.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"intransitive","wordFamily":["counterreform","misreform","reformability","reformable","reformate","reformee","Reformer","rereform","unreformability"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki